# Web Use Backends - Configuration Template

Copy this file to `backends.md` and fill in the env var names or 1Password references for your environment.

---

## Canonical roles

| Backend | Canonical role | Default? | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Lightweight retrieval (`web_search`, `web_fetch`, simple `browser`) | discovery, quick reads, easy public pages | Yes | always prefer when enough |
| Browserless (`/stealth/bql`, `/unblock`) | protected extraction plus programmable browser automation | Yes, for hard server-side extraction | strongest path for Cloudflare/bot-gated reads |
| Browserless (`/session`) | opt-in persistent protected extraction session | No | use sparingly only when cookies/localStorage/sessionStorage/cache must persist across repeated same-site BQL/CDP calls |
| TinyFish Browser API (CDP session) | remote stealth browser primitive for brittle or multi-step flows | Secondary / situational | works on protected pages when used as a browser session |
| Site-specific structured data API | clean structured fields for one site | No | domain skill owns credential, cost policy, and when it is worth a call |
| OpenClaw browser | interactive human-visible browsing | No | use for login, 2FA, manual review, or account state |

---

## Secrets

Fetch at runtime. **Never hardcode.**

Recommended pattern — 1Password:
```bash
op read "op://YOUR_VAULT/TinyFish API Key/credential"
op read "op://YOUR_VAULT/Browserless API Key/credential"
```

Or via environment variables:
```bash
export BROWSERLESS_TOKEN="..."
export TINYFISH_API_KEY="..."
```

`BROWSERLESS_API_KEY` is accepted as a legacy fallback by the local helper
scripts, but new config should use `BROWSERLESS_TOKEN`.

---

## Policy

### Browserless first for protected extraction
Prefer Browserless when the core problem is **getting through protection and extracting page data server-side**:
- Cloudflare or bot-gated pages
- protected pages that a normal fetch/headless browser cannot read
- structured extraction from a protected page

Default to stateless Browserless calls for one-off reads. Use Browserless `/session` only when repeated same-site work genuinely benefits from persisted cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage, or cache. Session responses include token-bearing `connect`, `browserQL`, and `stop` URLs; do not paste them into chat/logs, store local session files with 0600 permissions, and call the `stop` URL when done.

### TinyFish for browser-session control on hard pages
Prefer TinyFish when the core problem is **needing a remote stealth browser you can control**:
- brittle multi-step flows
- custom CDP / Playwright-style control
- cases where a higher-level agent run is too fuzzy but a full browser session helps

**Important:** TinyFish's **Agent API** is not the default for Cloudflare-protected extraction. The confirmed working path is its **Browser API / CDP session**.

### Site-specific structured APIs stay domain-owned
Use a structured API when the relevant domain skill says the clean fields and cost tradeoff are worth it. Keep API-specific credentials and policy in that domain skill, not here.

---

## Bundled helper usage

### Browserless helper

```bash
BROWSERLESS_TOKEN="<your token>" \
  python3 skills/web-use/scripts/browserless_extract.py \
  <URL> \
  --mode stealth-bql
```

Modes:
- `stealth-bql` — best default for protected extraction
- `unblock` — good for page HTML/content
- `content` — basic headless fetch

### Browserless session helper

Use sparingly. This is not the default extraction path.

```bash
BROWSERLESS_TOKEN="<your token>" \
  python3 skills/web-use/scripts/browserless_session.py create \
  --session-file /tmp/bl-session.json \
  --ttl-ms 1800000

python3 skills/web-use/scripts/browserless_session.py query \
  --session-file /tmp/bl-session.json \
  --query 'mutation Go($url: String!) { goto(url: $url, waitUntil: domContentLoaded) { status } body: text(selector: "body") { text } }' \
  --variables-json '{"url":"<URL>"}'

python3 skills/web-use/scripts/browserless_session.py stop \
  --session-file /tmp/bl-session.json \
  --delete-file
```

Do not paste the session file contents, `browserQL`, `connect`, or `stop` URLs into chat or logs. They include the Browserless token and should be treated as bearer credentials.

### Browserless media-request helper

```bash
BROWSERLESS_TOKEN="<your token>" \
  python3 skills/web-use/scripts/browserless_media_requests.py \
  <URL>
```

Use this when a page exposes media only after rendering or playback, such as a
public social post/Reel where `yt-dlp` failed. Returned CDN URLs are temporary;
store source URL, method, fetch time, and summarized media metadata by default.

### TinyFish helper

```bash
TINYFISH_API_KEY="<your key>" \
  python3 skills/web-use/scripts/tinyfish_browser_extract.py \
  <URL>
```

Creates a TinyFish browser session over CDP, navigates, waits, and returns normalized JSON.

---

## Endpoint reference

| Service | Endpoint |
|---|---|
| Browserless `/content` | `https://production-sfo.browserless.io/content?token=<TOKEN>` |
| Browserless `/unblock` | `https://production-sfo.browserless.io/unblock?token=<TOKEN>` |
| Browserless `/stealth/bql` | `https://production-sfo.browserless.io/stealth/bql?token=<TOKEN>` |
| Browserless `/session` | `https://production-sfo.browserless.io/session?token=<TOKEN>` |
| TinyFish Browser API | `https://api.browser.tinyfish.ai/` (X-API-Key header) |
| Site-specific structured API | See the relevant domain skill |

---

## Escalation order

1. Lightweight: `web_search` / `web_fetch` / simple `browser`
2. Browserless `/stealth/bql` or `/unblock`
3. TinyFish Browser API / CDP session
4. Site-specific structured API if the domain skill says it is worth it
5. Interactive browser (human-present)
6. Report blockage clearly

---

> **This is an example/template file.** Add your actual credentials and endpoints in your own `backends.md` (gitignored or kept local).
